<p>Pakistan&#x27;s government rolled back part of a sharp fuel-price increase and announced one month of free state-run public transport in Islamabad and Punjab after protests and long queues formed at petrol stations following a late-night price hike. Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif said the move was meant to ease pressure on households as the country faced an energy shock linked in the coverage to the Iran war. Reports said petrol had first risen about 42.7% to 485 Pakistani rupees per litre, drawing immediate public backlash. A day later, the government cut petrol by PKR 80 per litre and offered targeted subsidies in regions outside Islamabad and Punjab, a swift policy reversal aimed at containing public anger and softening the blow for commuters and lower-income residents.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/cOSxtelxPJsMAZTCroIU6xPCbIMTpF1PoSUF_iW44l_r9WF6osjT9Ow6bSBG7pbw_rrdAP_KF5OCk6K-D89ZvfyZ9GhxrrbDSRt1-_r3Bnl5tDYhU8CQKvZcoiiG_9q_2X4xvHRz9ZIuwnPCivQk9m6l1V1oVxB1Ab6Ht1pmpDsfVG18hTp0vvm9UBMYjpKNqY92dcBSB3RNnemxJLG-XhoUqMMw7xcSKQ4LDzzXel87fUvv6N-8g1gbv_gHvA' alt='Motorcyclists queue at a fuel station in Pakistan after the sharp price increase.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Diesel surge: The earlier government decision also raised high-speed diesel prices by 55%, a bigger jump than for petrol, according to The Hindu&#x27;s account of the pricing announcement.</li><li>Regional scope: The free-transport measure covered state-run systems in the capital and in Punjab, Pakistan&#x27;s most populous province, while other areas were promised targeted relief instead of universal free rides.</li><li>Rapid reversal: Sharif changed course within about a day of the initial price move, showing how quickly demonstrations and fuel-station queues turned into policy pressure.</li><li>No-cost transit: Officials said free public transport would run for 30 days, giving regular commuters immediate relief while the government manages wider fuel-cost pressures.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif: Sharif announced a partial fuel-price rollback and free transport after the backlash, presenting both as steps to reduce the burden on the public during the energy strain. (<a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/pakistan-pm-shehbaz-sharif-reduces-petrol-prices-announces-free-public-transport-amid-energy-crisis-iran-war-11309232#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>)</li><li>Protesters and motorists: Street demonstrators and people waiting in long motorcycle queues at petrol stations showed immediate public resistance to the overnight fuel-price increase. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/asia/south-asia/article/3349010/pakistan-offers-30-days-free-public-transport-following-40-petrol-hike'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Provincial and regional authorities: Authorities limited free transport to Islamabad and Punjab while offering targeted subsidies elsewhere, reflecting a more tailored relief approach across different parts of the country. (<a href='https://globalnation.inquirer.net/316792/pakistan-announces-free-public-transport-as-energy-crisis-bites'>Inquirer.net</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/pakistan-pm-shehbaz-sharif-reduces-petrol-prices-announces-free-public-transport-amid-energy-crisis-iran-war-11309232#publisher=newsstand'>Pak Reduces Petrol Prices, Announces Free Public Transport Amid Energy Crisis</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/apr/03/middle-east-crisis-live-trump-urges-iran-to-make-deal-after-bridge-strike'>Pakistan announces free public transport after fuel price hike – as it happened</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/pakistan-government-slashes-petrol-price-by-pkr-80-per-litre-day-after-sharp-hike-met-severe-backlash/article70822285.ece'>Pakistan government slashes petrol price by PKR 80 per litre, day after sharp hike met severe backlash</a> - thehindu.c